{
  /* The web host's hosting Worker — `app-worker/`, the third Cloudflare Worker here. Every
     compiler option below is `tsconfig.relay.json`'s byte for byte, and that file carries the
     argument for why a Worker gets a program of its own: `@cloudflare/workers-types` declares
     `Request`, `Response` and some four hundred other globals, and the app under `src/` must see
     the DOM's versions of those names and nothing else.

     A third file rather than a wider `include` in either of the other two, for
     `tsconfig.share-worker.json`'s reason: `mtg-grimoire-app` is a deploy of its own, and one
     program spanning two Workers lets an edit to one fail the other's check.

     **One file under `src/` is in this program, by import and not by name**:
     `src/lib/core/web/assets.ts`, whose `isNavigation` the Worker answers a navigation by — the
     rule the dev server and the preview serve by, shared so the three cannot disagree. It is
     followed from `app-worker/src/index.ts` and so checked a third time, under a third set of
     globals, which is the point: it may name nothing only one runtime has.

     `app-worker/src`'s `*.test.ts` are inside the program, as the relay's are: a test that does
     not type-check can drift from what it asserts about. `app-worker/src/vite.d.ts` declares the
     two Vite imports the fence reads the configuration and the Rust sources by, for
     `relay/src/raw.d.ts`'s reason — `vite/client` stays out of a Worker's `types`. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    /* No `DOM`: this is not a browser. */
    "lib": ["ES2022"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "types": ["@cloudflare/workers-types"],

    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "noEmit": true
  },
  "include": ["app-worker/src"]
}
